do-init-gate: ok — 3 governed hook(s) across 3 class(es) (1 per-request onStart, 1 container-start onStart, 1 SDK-awaited recovery); 5/5 wrangler-declared DO classes defined here and parsed; input blocks reach no named container RPC through local/virtual methods
  block-call graph is blind to computed names, imported helpers and indirectly passed callbacks
do-init-gate: blind to — what `classifyRecoveredFiber` (packages/cf-backend/src/fiber-recovery.ts:362) CALLS: this gate proves it is synchronous, and a synchronous function cannot await, but the arms' own
  discipline (hand every re-drive to a detached durable carrier, never join one) is held by packages/cf-backend/tests/unit-eviction-durability.test.ts, not here;
  recovery hooks outside `RECOVERY_HOOKS` — the set is pinned from the vendored agents/think chains, so a vendor bump that awaits a NEW subclass hook in the gate
  is ungoverned until the name is added here;
  what an onStart-spawned call REACHES beyond the 11 pinned `MODEL_SINKS` names: the rule is by NAME, so a helper spawned there that reaches a
  model under a name not on the list is ungoverned — and the recovery hooks are exempt from that rule outright, because their sanctioned answer hands a re-drive (which may
  reach the model) to a detached durable carrier;
  what `runStartHook` (packages/devbox/src/devbox.ts:936) CALLS past its own body: this gate proves it places direct container calls under the raced budget and finds a control-port proof.
  Indirect calls, runtime entry ordering, cancellation and data-size bounds need lifecycle tests.
